schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Eight Forms of Capital
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A framework naming eight forms of capital, financial, social, cultural,
    material, living, spiritual, experiential, and intellectual, used to argue
    that sending money alone doesn't grow the deep local roots that make
    regeneration last.
  source_traditions:
    - Ethan Roland and Gregory Landua (credited in the article as the framework's coiners)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used to justify why a local node's "unglamorous translation work" (relationships,
    language, cultural context) matters beyond just moving funding — the node is
    framed as what grows the seven non-financial forms of capital.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - subsidiarity (the governance principle that assigns this work to the local level)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Financial capital vs. the other seven — the article's point is
      specifically that Greenpill-style regen communities lean on this
      framework to avoid conflating "funded" with "regenerating."
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating the eight forms of capital as a checklist to tick off risks
    missing the article's actual point — that these forms are built through
    sustained local relationship-work, not through a program or a grant line
    item.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/why-local-matters.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
